WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.056 --> 00:00:13.216
Michelangelo was born in 1475 during the Renaissance, a period of time in history in which people began to rediscover the great artistic achievements of ancient cultures.

2
00:00:13.217 --> 00:00:20.374
It lasted for two hundred years, beginning in the fifteenth century, and was a profound awakening.

3
00:00:20.375 --> 00:00:28.963
People began to appreciate great art, the printing press was invented, and many major scientific discoveries were made.

4
00:00:28.964 --> 00:00:34.119
Michelangelo’s family was from a small town near Florence called Caprese.

5
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:38.610
His father was a nobleman, but was unable to hold a steady job.

6
00:00:38.611 --> 00:00:43.840
His mother was very ill and was unable to properly care for a baby.

7
00:00:43.841 --> 00:00:51.185
So, soon after his birth, Michelangelo’s parents sent him to be fostered by a local stonecutter’s wife.

8
00:00:51.186 --> 00:01:00.973
Later, when Michelangelo became famous, he would joke that his love for carving stone was a result of being nursed by this stonecutter’s wife.

9
00:01:00.974 --> 00:01:09.241
When Michelangelo was six years old, his mother died and his father remarried and moved the family to Florence.

10
00:01:09.242 --> 00:01:22.653
The young Michelangelo showed a keen interest in art, and eventually his father permitted him to join a bottega, which is a studio where young artists apprentice with a prominent master to learn the trade.

11
00:01:22.654 --> 00:01:31.558
At the bottega Michelangelo learned the technique of fresco painting, a skill that became very useful later in his life.

12
00:01:31.559 --> 00:01:39.154
This technique was very difficult to master and required the artist to paint quickly on wet plaster before it dried. This

13
00:01:39.155 --> 00:01:45.961
way the colors became part of the wall itself and would remain vivid for hundreds of years. In

14
00:01:45.962 --> 00:01:53.876
1490, when Michelangelo turned fifteen years old, he met a man who greatly changed his life. This

15
00:01:53.877 --> 00:02:02.773
man, Lorenzo de Medici, also known as Lorenzo the Magnificent, was a rich nobleman and ruler of Florence. He

16
00:02:02.774 --> 00:02:08.701
was also a great admirer of art and had a sculpture academy at his palace. Lorenzo

17
00:02:08.702 --> 00:02:14.938
immediately took a liking to the young talented artist and became his first patron. He

18
00:02:14.939 --> 00:02:20.023
invited Michelangelo to live at his palace and learn to sculpt marble. Michelangelo’s

19
00:02:20.024 --> 00:02:24.749
talent for sculpture soon became obvious to everyone. He

20
00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:30.264
was able to turn cold slabs of marble into amazingly lifelike figures. Unfortunately

21
00:02:30.265 --> 00:02:38.169
Michelangelo’s time at the Medici palace was cut short when Lorenzo died in 1492. Deeply

22
00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:44.827
saddened by his patron’s death, Michelangelo returned home to his father and three brothers. He

23
00:02:44.828 --> 00:02:49.833
continued to sculpt and became more and more interested in the human body. He

24
00:02:49.834 --> 00:02:55.227
often visited a local hospital and was allowed to study anatomy in the morgue. He

25
00:02:55.228 --> 00:03:02.147
would spend hours dissecting dead bodies, memorizing the positions of the veins, muscles, and bones. This

26
00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:08.400
was a difficult task, but it helped him to carve a realistic human from a block of stone. Life

27
00:03:08.401 --> 00:03:15.312
in Florence became unbearable for artists after Lorenzo de Medici’s son Piero came to power. He

28
00:03:15.313 --> 00:03:24.543
didn’t support the arts like his father and even made fun of Michelangelo’s talents by summoning him to the palace to make a snowman! Hurt,

29
00:03:24.544 --> 00:03:32.869
Michelangelo headed for Rome in 1497 in search of other patrons who would actually appreciate his talent. At

30
00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:40.791
that time Pope Julius II was the spiritual leader of the Roman Catholic Church and the ruler of Rome. He

31
00:03:40.792 --> 00:03:49.021
was a passionate patron of the arts who commissioned works from artists to build up the image of the Church and spread its message. The

32
00:03:49.022 --> 00:03:55.187
pope lived in a large palace, called the Vatican, and had his own private chapel. Michelangelo

33
00:03:55.188 --> 00:04:02.183
knew that he wanted to work for the pope because the church was very rich and powerful. In

34
00:04:02.184 --> 00:04:08.937
Rome Michelangelo met the man who would make him famous, Cardinal Jean de Lagraulas. The

35
00:04:08.938 --> 00:04:14.077
Cardinal commissioned the artist to make a statue for the Pope’s private chapel. Michelangelo

36
00:04:14.078 --> 00:04:19.919
created a marble sculpture of Mary holding the dead Christ in her arms. He

37
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:28.576
purposefully made Mary appear far too young to be Christ’s mother because he felt that she was so pure that she could never age. The

38
00:04:28.577 --> 00:04:33.830
statue became known as Pieta , which means "pity" in Italian. When

39
00:04:33.831 --> 00:04:40.073
Michelangelo’s Pieta was finished in 1500, he became the toast of Rome. Pilgrims

40
00:04:40.074 --> 00:04:49.892
flocked from all over Europe to see the sentimental figures, and nobody could believe that the sculptor who made them was just 25 years old. Some

41
00:04:49.893 --> 00:04:55.979
people even questioned whether Michelangelo really was the artist who made the sculpture. Enraged,

42
00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:04.137
Michelangelo snuck into the chapel late one night with his tools and carved his signature in Mary’s sash. To

43
00:05:04.138 --> 00:05:12.699
this day, if you look closely, you can see the words he carved in Italian: "Michelangelo from Florence made this." During

44
00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:19.707
his time in Rome, Michelangelo’s friends wrote to him that life in Florence was becoming better. Piero

45
00:05:19.708 --> 00:05:28.524
de Medici was gone, and Florence was now an independent republic with no rich family or religious leader ruling the city. Once

46
00:05:28.525 --> 00:05:33.368
again Florence was becoming a flourishing place for artists. Michelangelo

47
00:05:33.369 --> 00:05:41.344
decided to return and was soon commissioned to sculpt a large block of marble for the main square of the city. He

48
00:05:41.345 --> 00:05:48.025
chose to portray the figure of David just before his battle with the evil giant Goliath. It

49
00:05:48.026 --> 00:05:54.437
symbolized the determination of the people of Florence to survive during difficult times. The

50
00:05:54.438 --> 00:06:01.167
sculpture was 17 feet high and showed Michelangelo’s remarkable knowledge of human anatomy. He

51
00:06:01.168 --> 00:06:06.167
worked with no assistants and didn’t even make a clay model first. He

52
00:06:06.168 --> 00:06:10.905
attacked the giant piece of marble with rigorous energy and passion. Michelangelo

53
00:06:10.906 --> 00:06:18.978
was already well known for his Pieta , but after he completed David , he became truly

54
00:06:18.979 --> 00:06:25.477
famous. Upon hearing of his successes, Pope Julius II summoned Michelangelo back to

55
00:06:25.478 --> 00:06:32.063
Rome. He wanted to commission the artist to create a masterpiece that would serve as a monument to his

56
00:06:32.064 --> 00:06:40.624
fame. He wanted Michelangelo to paint the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel, the most prestigious and important place in the pope’s

57
00:06:40.625 --> 00:06:47.956
palace. The Sistine Chapel was already beautifully decorated by other artists, but its ceiling was not very

58
00:06:47.957 --> 00:06:51.630
interesting. It was pale blue and covered with gold

59
00:06:51.631 --> 00:06:55.297
stars. Michelangelo was nervous about this new

60
00:06:55.298 --> 00:07:04.607
task. He considered himself a sculptor, not a painter, and the only experience he had in fresco painting was the time he had spent in the

61
00:07:04.608 --> 00:07:09.781
bottega. But nobody could argue with the pope, so Michelangelo set to

62
00:07:09.782 --> 00:07:17.434
work. First, he sketched out a design for the entire ceiling, breaking it down into nine individual biblical

63
00:07:17.435 --> 00:07:23.773
scenes. Then, after six months of preparation, Michelangelo was ready to start painting the

64
00:07:23.774 --> 00:07:31.754
ceiling. Michelangelo spent the next four years sixty feet up in the air on top of scaffolding, painting the

65
00:07:31.755 --> 00:07:38.746
ceiling. He hired five assistants to help him, but fired them soon after because they did not meet his high standards.

66
00:07:38.747 --> 00:07:43.669
He worked standing up, bent over backward in a very uncomfortable position.

67
00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:47.582
He barely ate, only enough to keep his body going.

68
00:07:47.583 --> 00:07:54.828
Often he would collapse in bed with his clothes on, only to get up again the next morning and return straight to work.

69
00:07:54.829 --> 00:07:59.406
After four exhausting years, the ceiling was complete.

70
00:07:59.407 --> 00:08:05.157
On October 31, 1512, the Sistine Chapel was opened to the public.

71
00:08:05.158 --> 00:08:09.233
Everyone was astonished by Michelangelo’s masterpiece.

72
00:08:09.234 --> 00:08:16.058
He was able to portray, in nine separate panels, the biblical story of creation and destruction.

73
00:08:16.059 --> 00:08:23.049
There are three hundred figures painted so realistically that they look as if they will spring out of the painting.

74
00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:29.049
The Sistine Chapel ceiling is considered to be one of the artistic wonders of the world.

75
00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:41.371
Michelangelo spent the rest of his life creating memorable sculptures and painting more frescoes, but he would be remembered most for his masterpiece on the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel.

76
00:08:41.372 --> 00:08:46.368
Michelangelo died in 1564 at the age of 89.

77
00:08:46.369 --> 00:08:52.296
He had lived a full and turbulent life, creating some of the most astounding works of art ever seen.

78
00:08:52.297 --> 00:09:00.423
He never stopped challenging himself through his art, and as a result, he left a lasting legacy in his paintings and sculptures.

